# carts/shmup — scripted walkthrough. Hand-written (unlike carts/snake's golden.input,
# which is generated by a planner): every enemy in this cartridge is stationary, so hitting
# one is a question of parking the ship under its column and firing, not of tracking motion.
#
# Grammar (quarp replay record --input-file, InputScript.cs): tick:buttons, entries
# separated by commas or newlines, '#' starts a comment. Buttons are held from one entry
# until the next, so every tap (movement release, single shot) needs an explicit follow-up
# entry with an empty button list.
#
# What happens, tick by tick, against src/main.cs's own constants (see also
# replays/README.md for the full arithmetic this schedule is built from):
#
#   ticks 1-21    hold Left:  ship drifts from its start X=60 to X=39 — wave 0's column 0.
#   tick 25       tap O: one shot. It clears the AABB gate at spawn+19 == tick 44 (row 0,
#                 y=16); wave 0's own column-0 enemy is not scheduled to fire until tick 110,
#                 so it never gets the chance.
#   ticks 30-43   hold Right: X 39 -> 53, column 1.
#   tick 47       tap O; column-1 enemy dies at tick 66 (scheduled to fire at 113 — again,
#                 dead well before its own trigger tick).
#   ticks 55-68   hold Right: X 53 -> 67, column 2.
#   tick 72       tap O; column-2 enemy dies at tick 91 (its own fire tick is 116).
#   ticks 80-93   hold Right: X 67 -> 81, column 3 — the last of wave 0's four enemies.
#   tick 97       tap O; column-3 enemy dies at tick 116, one tick before its own scheduled
#                 fire at 119. Wave 0 (4 enemies, all of them) is fully shot down, and not one
#                 of its four ever got a shot off — the ship takes zero damage through the
#                 whole engagement.
#
#   Ship then sits idle at X=81 through the rest of wave 0's clock, all of wave 1's
#   intermission and combat window (columns 46/60/74 never reach X=81, so this is safe by
#   construction — see replays/README.md), and wave 2's 90-tick intermission.
#
#   tick 670      wave 2 goes active (its own intermission ends here); 8 enemies (4x2), none
#                 shot at, all still alive. Their first-volley fire schedule is
#                 670 + 20 + 3*i for spawn index i = 0..7, i.e. 690, 693, ..., 711.
#   tick 711      the 8th and last enemy of the opening volley fires. At this exact tick all
#                 8 wave-2 enemies are alive (nothing has touched them) and all 8 of their
#                 first-volley bullets are alive (the oldest, fired at 690, has travelled only
#                 21 of its ~48-tick lifetime) — the >=8-bullets/>=4-enemies load point the
#                 work order asks for. No RNG is involved in reaching this tick: see the doc
#                 comment on UpdateIntermission in src/main.cs.
#   ticks 712-730 hold Right: X 81 -> 100, out of every wave-2 column's bullet path (columns
#                 sit at 39/53/67/81; a bullet's hitbox tops out at column-X+2+3=86 px, well
#                 short of 100). The earliest any wave-2 bullet could have reached the ship's
#                 row is tick 725 (column 0) — the dodge finishes at 730, but the only column
#                 that ever shared the ship's X (column 3, at X=81) doesn't arrive until 734
#                 and 736, both after the ship has already left. Zero damage here too.
#   tick 870      wave 2's 200-tick clock runs out; it was the last wave, so the run ends in
#                 a win. The ship never lost a life the entire way — 3 lives spent, 0.
#
# Recorded/played with:
#   quarp replay record carts/shmup -o carts/shmup/replays/walkthrough.qrpr --ticks 900 \
#       --input-file carts/shmup/replays/walkthrough.input --every 20
1:L,22:,25:O,26:,30:R,44:,47:O,48:,55:R,69:,72:O,73:,80:R,94:,97:O,98:,712:R,731:
